<h1>DonationTracker</h1>

<p class="lead">
    A small ASP.NET Core MVC app that records supporters and the donations they
    make, and keeps a running campaign total.
</p>

<h2 class="h4">What it does</h2>
<ul>
    <li><a asp-controller="Supporters" asp-action="Index">Supporters</a>: list, add and edit the people who donate.</li>
    <li><a asp-controller="Donations" asp-action="Index">Donations</a>: list, add and edit donations, each linked to a supporter, with the campaign total at the top.</li>
    <li><a href="/api/donations">GET /api/donations</a>: the most recent donations as JSON.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Every form is validated in the browser and again on the server. A donation
    cannot be dated in the future and must belong to an existing supporter.
</p>

<h2 class="h4">How it was built</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>ASP.NET Core MVC on .NET 10</strong>, with Razor views, tag helpers and the template's Bootstrap.</li>
    <li><strong>Entity Framework Core and SQL Server LocalDB</strong>, with the schema created by a single migration.</li>
    <li><strong>Three layers</strong>: controllers call a service, the service calls a repository, and only the repository uses the database context.</li>
    <li><strong>Business rules in the service</strong>, so they can be unit tested without a database.</li>
    <li><strong>NUnit and Moq tests</strong> for the service, using a mocked repository.</li>
    <li><strong>Hand-written SQL</strong>: a stored procedure and a covering index, measured before and after with execution plans (402 logical reads down to 4).</li>
    <li><strong>A JSON API</strong> that returns a DTO, never the database entity.</li>
</ul>

